welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Louisa Lombard an assistant professor  of anthropology she's a cultural  anthropologist who studies African  borderland areas where the state is  largely absent and a range of actors  govern professor Lombards research  locales primarily the remote and little  populated eastern reaches of the Central  African Republic are further marked by  violent histories that continue into the  present  her main fieldwork interlocutors are  among the region's men in arms such as  anti poaching guards and rebels today  we'll talk with professor Lombard about  her new book state of rebellion violence  and intervention in the Central African  Republic  welcome professor Longworth thanks  Marilyn let's start with an overview of  your book tell us about it yeah so  really the objective of this book was  that you know this this country the  Central African Republic which I've been  studying since 2003 really began to  descend into war in late 2012 into 2013  and things got really bad there for a  while and I you know this is a country  that has shaped me as a person and as a  scholar and I was following closely  everything that was going on in the  country and I wanted to tell the story  of this conflict of what had been going  on during the war in a way that would  draw out not just the kind of  blow-by-blow of everything that was  happening on a daily basis but that  would also bring in some of the  historical and social factors that had  gone into creating the situation that we  found in the country and it would also  do a little bit of a kind of structural  analysis of the position of central  Africa in the world and one of the  things I realized in telling that story  was that first of all this case of the  Central African Republic actually draws  out a lot of problems with the usual  ways that we understand conflict in  Africa and so I wanted to you know  critique some of those usual ways of  thinking  about conflict in Africa and also  proposed an alternative way of thinking  about understanding and analyzing what's  going on in these places okay because it  seemed to me that you know there have  been some really big shifts in terms of  how conflict happens in Africa today and  one of those is the increased role of  international organizations the UN big  UN missions NGOs all of these kinds of  of actors and so it's really gotten to  the point now where it no longer makes  sense to try to analyze or think about  conflict on the continent as if it were  the product of these sort of bounded  cultures of rebel groups on the one hand  and then you know government actors on  the other hand and international actors  on you know on another third hand if you  happen to have that many what we really  need to understand is how these  different actors are becoming entangled  with each other and developing  relationships with each other that  structure what they're able to do and  what they're not able to do and so  that's some of what I try to do in the  book some of the early sections of the  book talk about sort of what the state  has been in Central African Republic in  the role of violence in it and the  reasons why the kind of label of failed  state although in some sense evocative  of the kind of dysfunction that you find  is analytically not very helpful in  terms of understanding either what has  been happening or what we should do to  try to fix it and then talk about  specific forms of international  intervention that we've seen through the  years disarmament programs and the like  and talk about also the specific forms  of violence that we saw during the war  and sort of where they come from and  some of what they need okay  so I know you spent a lot of time in the  field in central Africa how did you do  your research so this book really grows  out of my research and engagement in  Central African Republic since 2003 so  that's been a few years at this point  and as a cultural anthropologist my  methods are qualitative so I do a range  of things participant observation  interviews hang out with people  participate in meetings all of that kind  of thing so I've  a lot of time you know hanging out with  members of rebel groups and interviewing  them going to United Nations meetings  all of these different kinds of things  and having this perspective of having  done research in the country for you  know a few years at this point has made  it possible for me to track both some of  what has changed during that time and  then also what remains the same and I'm  able to kind of look for patterns and  see patterns and see what is persisting  and what is changing and and what that  really means I'm wondering as a woman if  you were ever you know fearful for  yourself especially being in the field  with rebels did you ever have any kind  of situations that arise where you  didn't feel safe that's a good question  I would say no I could have put myself  into situations that would have been  very scary but I tend to be pretty  careful and I you know do a lot of  groundwork before I go anywhere talking  to different people about what the  situation is like and making sure that  if I go as a researcher who wants to ask  people questions that this will be  welcomed that this will not be taken as  an affront and I always work through  chains of command and hierarchies to  make sure that people know who I am what  I'm doing you know we have luckily here  at Yale an informed consent process or  an ethics overview and I make sure to  follow all of those rules I've been able  to stay pretty safe okay great now you  you spoke of things having changed let's  let's talk about 20 years ago to today  what changes have you seen and why and  also what things have stayed the same so  one of the big changes is that the  region has become much more militarized  and this was starting already more than  20 years ago some of the wars in the in  the region such as in Sudan South Sudan  chad conflict in most places has been  going on for a very long time but it's  really intensified and it's difficult to  summarize the reasons for why that's  happened but the situation that we find  today is that Central African Republic  unfortunately we don't have a map here  but if you can pick  sure it all of its neighbors it's this  landlocked country and all of its  neighbors either have recently been at  war or have high levels of conflict you  know we're talking about Chad Sudan  South Sudan Democratic Republic of the  Congo Republic of Congo I used to say  that Cameroon was the one exception but  now Cameron has seen a lot of conflict  as well with Boko Haram and and related  developments and then Central African  Republic has its own conflict dynamics  so there's been this kind of ramping up  of the militarization of the region and  in concert with that there has been a  ramping up of kind of international  interventions to try to manage and deal  with these conflicts primarily done by  the African Union the the United Nations  but also taking the form of lots of aid  organizations are they helping at all  I think they're doing a lot to help in a  kind of band a band aid type of a way I  don't think that they are really  tackling some of the structural problems  that exist for this country or this  region what are the structural problems  so I think the main structural problem  is that the Central African Republic is  this incredibly improbable state it's  this vast vast area with very few people  and that has always been incredibly poor  during the colonial period as today and  yet we expect it to kind of look like a  state in this pretty typical sort of vey  Berrien model it just can't work it  won't work so we kind of we infuse a  number of international troops in the  form of peacekeeping missions and that  sort of keeps a lid on things for a  while but once they leave the the  fundamental problem is still there that  this is this poor and very weak state  that realistically is going to remain  that way for a very long time unless we  really start thinking differently about  what we might do in this so what are  some of the things that have stayed the  same some of the things that have stayed  the same that's a good question I mean  one unfortunately as I said is this sort  of structural problem of what the  Central African proud Republic is in the  world that it is this very  kind of weak state are a state that has  very little administrative capacity that  is very poor that is very large that has  very little infrastructure and if we  went back 50 years in time again the  region wasn't as militarized and it  wasn't as much of a problem it was kind  of a sleepy backwater um you know people  people liked it for that reason but as  the region has become much more  militarized it's become more and more of  a problem what are the reasons for  becoming more and more militarized I  mean what is going on there that causes  such strife versus having it be peaceful  well I think one factor I mean now I'll  just talk about Central African Republic  and I'm gonna leave out other countries  in the region but one factor was that  during the 1980s the country was in an  economic plummet and one of the  proposals by different international  actors the World Bank those kinds of  people was to say well what you really  need to do is become more efficient as a  government trim the civil service stop  hiring people moved to multiparty  democracy and elections all of these  good things and one consequence of that  was that it meant that the number of  people who were able to materially  benefit from the state was being  constrained and couldn't be expanded and  people started to feel left out and one  way that they had to protest and to say  no we need to be taken seriously is by  taking up arms and so it started with  army mutinies in the 1990s with members  of the with soldiers saying we're not  getting paid this is crazy  and then turned into a number of  rebellions I'm also all taking this this  form of saying no we need to be taken  seriously and being taken seriously  means I you know giving us some kind of  material dignity in addition to  everything else so that's that's one set  of factors  something that really interested me in  your book is the role the occults plays  so if you could talk a little bit about  that and how it's affected you know  Central Africa yeah so this is a big  thing inside  Africa and we could use a variety of  different words to talk about it the  occult witchcraft sorcery is sort of a  local term the problem with all of this  terminology is at the moment that we  hear the word witchcraft we start  thinking pointy hats brooms all of these  different kinds of things and that  doesn't really describe the whole sort  of world of witchcraft in central Africa  I think at the most fundamental level  we're talking about the occult or  witchcraft in central Africa we're  really talking about is that there's a  sort of widespread understanding that  the world consists of there's kind of  two worlds one is visible and sensible  and the other is invisible but people  can still be active in that kind of  invisible world and through that  invisible world can have an effect on  you and your and your life and the  understanding for a lot of people in  central Africa is that basically every  person has the capacity to become really  powerful in terms of witchcraft and use  that for their own betterment for their  own advancement and also to keep other  people back but most people don't do  anything with it most people just let it  lie they don't want to get involved in  that kind of thing but some people work  to kind of develop that capacity and  when they do that can have very you know  effects that people see as very  antisocial again such as you know making  it so that one person gets ahead but  usually in getting ahead somebody else  they feel like somebody else is getting  behind and so when people make recourse  to witchcraft saying it was because of  rich crafts that that guy is doing so  much better  in part it's an attempt it's a way of  assigning responsibility and  accountability for what people perceive  to be social ills and social wrongs okay  do do you think people use it I will say  inappropriately to cast aspersions on  another person purposely that definitely  happens luckily there are a range of  different criteria that have to be that  have to obtain for something - for  people to believe that accusation okay  and so people will be accused of  witchcraft and it will be found that  that accusation was baseless and  and you know the person who accused will  be the one who will lose face in that  situation doesn't always work that way  you know there are people who are  accused and if you went back and looked  at the evidence when you're in a sort of  cooler headed frame of mind you might  think differently from when you're in  the thick of the accusation but there is  some you know there are some parameters  for this that okay that keep it from  getting totally out of hand okay help me  understand when you say witchcraft  how does someone develop their  witchcraft in terms of getting stronger  I mean do they do ceremonies what  actually happens so often times they  would involve apprentice thing to  somebody who is a known sorcerer and  learning from that person and a lot of  how what goes on there is secret it's  not something that those of us who want  to claim that we are not witches and  don't know about witchcraft  not something that we would want to know  okay but it does involve various plants  that can have both medicinal and then  also kind of occult function in a range  of things okay interesting so what do  you think central Africa needs in terms  of moving the country forward well  unfortunately I think that the list of  things it needs are is long but let me  just mention two things today okay one  is that you know right now in the  country we have all of these people lots  of smart people both central Africans  and people from elsewhere trying to work  on the country's problems and trying to  make things better the problem is that  all of them have a very short-term kind  of lens for their engagement with the  country so if you're working for the UN  any project that you can push through is  going to have a funding cycle of maybe  two years maybe three years something  along those lines um so it's only ever  going to be this very short-term kind of  thing if you're a Central African  politician for a variety of reasons you  also tend to be thinking in a very  short-term kind of a way and the problem  is that the problems in central Africa  are long-term problems and they require  a real long term vision leadership  somebody who is going to sort of take  the reins and say we're gonna push this  or word and think in that long-term kind  of a way and be able to implement things  in a long-term kind of a way so there  needs to be some kind of fundamental  rethinking of how we give assistance to  this country and the way that we do it  so that's one set of problems that I  think we really need to grapple with and  figure out and then I also think that  you know there's a lot of emphasis on  sort of rebuilding the state in central  Africa well as I mentioned earlier the  state has never really had much of a  presence in in Central African Republic  it's always been very poor and hasn't  been great at providing services to  citizens so what if we sort of stop  focusing on the state as service  provider and instead start thinking  about what the state might be able to do  in terms of assuring that people have  some very basic level of material  dignity give people some money and then  see what they do with that right  basically I think that we need to work  more in the direction of a politics of  distribution and of giving the central  Africans that kind of rightful share you  know some not a lot of money but some  small amount that kind of is a way of  creating some accountability between  their leaders and and the citizens in  the country and that also goes a long  way toward just providing the kind of  grist for the small-scale kind of inter  economic transactions on a village level  on a town level that really get things  going okay so what would you like a  reader to take away from your book well  you know in the conclusion of the book I  quote a peanuts comic strip that was  actually first quoted by a great  anthropologist Clifford Geertz  and he quoted in the context of research  that he was doing in the 1960s and what  were then called the new States you know  sort of the post-colonial world and in  this comic strip Lucy says to Charlie  Brown she says you know what the problem  is with you Charlie Brown the problem  with you is that you are you and Charlie  Brown sort of looks at her I was like  well what am I supposed to do about that  Lizzie says I don't provide solutions I  just get to the you know  diagnose the root causes of the problem  and that comic-strip really resonated  with me because I think you know as an  academic as a researcher it's easy to  just kind of critique and point out the  problems but there are a lot of people  out there people who are working in  Central Africa now who are faced with  these really big problems and yet have  to try to figure out some way to move  forward some way to you know make make  decisions about about how we can try to  do something for this country and so you  know I really hope that people in  reading my book you know one of the  things I tried to do is sort of show you  know sort of readjust realign some of  the the frameworks that we bring to bear  to try to understand a place like that  like this and in doing so to provoke for  both people to think about things  differently provoke people to perhaps  come up with some different ideas for  what we might be able to do for this  country because ultimately you know my  hope is that this book will be you know  some kind of a testament to just the the  the the the challenges and privations  privations that so many central Africans  we've been so generous with their time  and and other things with me through the  years have to have to deal with and  it'll help us perhaps you know that  perhaps out of the critique we will also  find ways to perhaps a shore at least a  slightly more dignified future for  central Africans in the years to come  ok this has been very interesting thanks  so much for being here and thank you  Marilyn oh you're welcome  for more information about Professor  Lombard and her research please visit  our website at macmillan report yale.edu  be sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through the funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at you  [Music]  you

