fricans that kind of rightful share you  know some not a lot of money but some  small amount that kind of is a way of  creating some accountability between  their leaders and and the citizens in  the country and that also goes a long  way toward just providing the kind of  grist for the small-scale kind of inter  economic transactions on a village level  on a town level that really get things  going okay so what would you like a  reader to take away from your book well  you know in the conclusion of the book I  quote a peanuts comic strip that was  actually first quoted by a great  anthropologist Clifford Geertz  and he quoted in the context of research  that he was doing in the 1960s and what  were then called the new States you know  sort of the post-colonial world and in  this comic strip Lucy says to Charlie  Brown she says you know what the problem  is with you Charlie Brown the problem  with you is that you are you and Charlie  Brown sort of looks at her I was like  well what am I supposed to do about that  Lizzie says I don't provide solutions I  just get to the you know  diagnose the root causes of the problem  and that comic-strip really resonated  with me because I think you know as an  academic as a researcher it's easy to  just kind of critique and point out the  problems but there are a lot of people  out there people who are working in  Central Africa now who are faced with  these really big problems and yet have  to try to figure out some way to move  forward some way to you know make make  decisions about about how we can try to  do something for this country and so you  know I really hope that people in  reading my book you know one of the  things I tried to do is sort of show you  know sort of readjust realign some of  the the frameworks that we bring to bear  to try to understand a place like that  like this and in doing so to provoke for  both people to think about things  differently provoke people to perhaps  come up with some different ideas for  what we might be able to do for this  country because ultimately you know my  hope is that this book will be you know  some kind of a testament to just the the  the the the challenges and privations  privations that so many central Africans  we've been so generous with their time  and and other things with me through the  years have to have to deal with and  it'll help us perhaps you know that  perhaps out of the critique we will also  find ways to perhaps a shore at least a  slightly more dignified future for  central Africans in the years to come  ok this has been very interesting thanks  so much for being here and thank you  Marilyn oh you're welcome  for more information about Professor  Lombard and her research please visit  our website at macmillan report yale.edu  be sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through the funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at you  [Music]  you
 

